1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,425
How Group Chats Rebuilt the Kampong for Singapore's Seniors

2
00:00:04,425 --> 00:00:25,225
Somewhere in Singapore right now, a 78-year-old is checking a family WhatsApp group before breakfast, sending a good-morning sticker to a chat of ex-colleagues, or reading updates from her Active Ageing Centre's exercise group — quietly maintaining a social circle that, a decade ago, would have shrunk along with her mobility.

3
00:00:25,225 --> 00:00:38,875
It's an unglamorous kind of connection, but it's doing real work: a government push to get seniors online has ended up rebuilding something closer to old kampong-style closeness than anyone quite planned for.

4
00:00:38,875 --> 00:00:49,325
The programme behind it is called Seniors Go Digital, launched in June 2020 as the pandemic made digital exclusion suddenly urgent.

5
00:00:49,325 --> 00:01:14,425
Community clubs had actually been trying versions of this for decades — Marine Parade set up Singapore's first CC computer club back in 1980 — but Seniors Go Digital scaled it into something else entirely: more than 1,000 Digital Ambassadors deployed islandwide, teaching seniors not just how to use Singpass or book a doctor's appointment, but how to message, video call, and join group chats.

6
00:01:14,425 --> 00:01:23,475
Over 190,000 seniors have been trained so far, up from about 36,000 in the programme's first year.

7
00:01:23,475 --> 00:01:37,225
A companion scheme, Mobile Access for Seniors, subsidised smartphones and data plans for more than 13,000 lower-income seniors who otherwise wouldn't have had a device to train on at all.

8
00:01:37,225 --> 00:01:51,925
The problem it was built to solve is a real one: a Singapore study of nearly 6,000 seniors aged 68 to 77 found that 46.5% showed signs of social isolation.

9
00:01:51,925 --> 00:02:11,600
What's less obvious is that teaching someone to use a smartphone doesn't automatically fix that — a local trial found digital-literacy training alone often fell short, simply because a senior's existing friends and family weren't online either, so there was no one to actually talk to on the other end.

10
00:02:11,600 --> 00:02:29,525
The fix that worked, in a related programme called Project Wire Up, was explicitly three-part: hand over a smartphone, train the person to use it, and then deliberately reconnect them to their own social network — not just teach the skill, but rebuild the community around it.

11
00:02:29,525 --> 00:02:45,150
A separate Singapore study following that same logic found the mediation runs in a clear line: digital literacy leads to stronger social connectedness, which leads to less isolation, which shows up as measurably better wellbeing.

12
00:02:45,150 --> 00:02:55,675
That's the quieter story behind all those senior citizens tapping away in coffee shops and community clubs — it was never really about the smartphone.

13
00:02:55,675 --> 00:03:04,025
It was about giving a generation that had started losing its social circles a new, unglamorous way to hold onto them.

14
00:03:04,025 --> 00:03:20,800
Why it matters today: a programme built to close a "digital divide" ended up doing something more specific — rebuilding the kind of tight, everyday social contact that public housing and smaller families had quietly eroded, one group chat at a time.
